<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hippocrates of Gela</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 498–491 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippocrates">Hippocrates</a> of Gela was tyrant of the Greek city of Gela in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> from about 498 BCE. He built the first great territorial power in Greek Sicily, subduing Naxos, Leontini and other cities and defeating Syracuse, from which he took Kamarina and refounded it. On his death his cavalry commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelon">Gelon</a> seized power and later moved to Syracuse.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chian writers and thinkers, among them Ion, <b>Hippocrates</b>, the astronomer Oenopides and the philosopher Metrodorus, contributed to Greek poetry, mathematics and science.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Gela reached its peak under the tyrants Cleander and <b>Hippocrates</b>, who around 495 BCE dominated much of eastern Sicily, and under Gelon, who seized Syracuse in 485 BCE and moved half of Gela's people there.</blockquote><a href="/state/gela">Gela</a></li><li><blockquote>Kamarina repeatedly defied its mother city: it revolted around 553 BCE and was destroyed, was refounded by <b>Hippocrates of Gela</b> around 492 BCE, emptied again by Gelon in 484 BCE and refounded from Gela in 461 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/kamarina">Kamarina</a></li><li><blockquote>The city fell under <b>Hippocrates of Gela</b> around 495 BCE and later under Syracuse.</blockquote><a href="/state/leontini">Leontini</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hippocrates of Gela</b> subdued it around 495 BCE, and later it was often allied with Athens and Leontini against Syracuse.</blockquote><a href="/state/naxos-sicily">Naxos (Sicily)</a></li></ul></section>
